# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/002_integer_promotions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_integer_promotions
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Integer promotions of narrow types in expressions and as operands, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every operand is promoted to int before the operation, so no result leaves the range of
int and no signed type overflows: 200*200 and 300*300 are 40000 and 90000, negating -128
and -300 yields 128 and 300, and subtracting 1 from -128 yields -129, all representable.
The only shift count is the literal 20, strictly less than the 32-bit width of the
promoted operand, so the shift is defined; no negative value is ever shifted left.
No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no object modified twice between sequence
points, at most one side-effecting argument per call, no dependence on padding bytes.
The _Generic controlling expressions are unevaluated, so they report the promoted type
without performing any conversion at run time; unary + is what applies the promotions.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Plain char is never used as an arithmetic type and no object is declared plain char,
because plain char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on
AArch64 and RISC-V 64; explicit signed char and unsigned char are used instead. The only
character data is the format-string literals, whose bytes printf copies through unchanged;
no plain-char value is read as a number, converted, compared or formatted numerically, so
that difference cannot reach the output. long and unsigned long are never
used, because their width is 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the other three targets.
No pointer value is printed. int is 32 bits on all four supported targets, so every
promoted-width result above is stable across backends.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_uchar_product=40000
fold_ushort_product=90000
fold_uchar_complement=-1
fold_schar_negate=128
fold_short_negate=300
fold_uchar_shift=1048576
fold_schar_widen=-129
run_uchar_product=40000
run_ushort_product=90000
run_uchar_complement=-1
run_schar_negate=128
run_short_negate=300
run_uchar_shift=1048576
run_schar_widen=-129
promo_uchar=int
promo_schar=int
promo_ushort=int
promo_short=int
promo_bool=int
promo_product=int
END
